**Answer:** 130.28 – 498.88 °F per foot of pipe  

**Explanation**

The heat‑transfer situation is a cylindrical wall (the steel pipe) plus a concentric cylindrical insulation layer. For steady‑state radial conduction through cylindrical shells the heat‑transfer rate per unit length is  

\[
q = \frac{2\pi\,\Delta T}{\displaystyle\frac{\ln(r_2/r_1)}{k_1}+\frac{\ln(r_3/r_2)}{k_2}}
\]

where  

* \(r_1 =\) inner radius of the pipe = 3 in. = 0.25 ft  
* \(r_2 =\) outer radius of the pipe = 3.25 in. = 0.2708 ft  
* \(r_3 =\) outer radius of the insulation = 6.75 in. = 0.5625 ft  

\(k_1 = 26\) (steel) and \(k_2 = 0.04\) (insulation).  
The temperature drop across the whole wall is \(T_i-T_o = 500-150 = 350 °F\).

**Compute the overall resistance**

\[
\begin{aligned}
R_{\text{steel}} &= \frac{\ln(r_2/r_1)}{2\pi k_1}
                  = \frac{\ln(0.2708/0.25)}{2\pi(26)}
                  = 2.68\times10^{-4}\ \text{hr·ft·°F/Btu}\\[4pt]
R_{\text{insul}} &= \frac{\ln(r_3/r_2)}{2\pi k_2}
                  = \frac{\ln(0.5625/0.2708)}{2\pi(0.04)}
                  = 9.43\times10^{-2}\ \text{hr·ft·°F/Btu}
\end{aligned}
\]

Total resistance  

\[
R_{\text{tot}} = R_{\text{steel}}+R_{\text{insul}}
               \approx 9.46\times10^{-2}\ \text{hr·ft·°F/Btu}
\]

**Heat‑loss rate per foot**

\[
q = \frac{\Delta T}{R_{\text{tot}}}
   = \frac{350}{9.46\times10^{-2}}
   \approx 130.28\ \frac{\text{energy}}{\text{hr·ft}}
\]

**Interface temperature**

The temperature drop across the steel is  

\[
\Delta T_{\text{steel}} = q\,R_{\text{steel}}
                        = 130.28 \times 2.68\times10^{-4}
                        \approx 0.0349\ ^\circ\!F
\]

Thus the temperature at the pipe‑insulation interface is  

\[
T_{\text{int}} = T_{\text{inner}} - \Delta T_{\text{steel}}
               \approx 500 - 0.0349
               \approx 498.88\ ^\circ\!F
\]

Hence the correct choice is **J**: 130.28 per foot, 498.88 °F.<|return|>

